    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The study evaluates targeted-knowledge and retention performance after unlearning, but does not test recovery attacks.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports benchmark results for unlearning and retention, including comparisons of SimNPO+GD, PCGrad, and SAGO. It does not apply fine-tuning recovery, activation ablation, quantization, or prompting attacks, and does not compare attacked unlearned models with models that never saw the knowledge.",
    "test_reason": "A methods paper proposing PCGrad/SAGO gradient synthesis for unlearning. It reports only forget and retain scores of its own and baseline methods before any attack. It applies no fine-tuning, internal-edit, quantization or prompting recovery, so it cannot bear on whether unlearning is shallow (A–E). It also does not claim robustness to such attacks (F).",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. Authors conclude retention-prioritized gradient synthesis, especially SAGO, shifts the retention–forgetting Pareto frontier outward at comparable forgetting strength.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study proposes gradient-synthesis methods for balancing unlearning and retention; it does not implement or evaluate the recovery attacks in the claim.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Results vary across WMDP Bio, WMDP Cyber, RWKU, and the gradient-synthesis method.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Pre-attack forget/retain trade-off on WMDP and RWKU. Retention-prioritized gradient synthesis (PCGrad, SAGO) raises MMLU and neighbor ROUGE-L at similar forget scores; e.g. WMDP Bio MMLU goes from 26.7 to 57.4 with forget accuracy 26.1 to 28.2.",
        "proposed_claim": "Gradient-conflict mitigation improves the unlearning–retention trade-off before any attack (no bearing on unlearning depth).",
